      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-theme-primary mb-4">2. How Construction Differences Affect Performance</h2>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4 leading-relaxed">
        Understanding <em>why</em> each mesh behaves the way it does will help you predict problems before they happen. The tradeoffs are real, and neither product is "better"—they solve different problems.
      </p>

      <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-theme-primary mb-3">Welded Wire Mesh: Strengths and Weaknesses</h3>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4 leading-relaxed">
        In welded mesh, longitudinal and transverse wires are laid in a grid and fused at each intersection by resistance welding. The result is a sheet that behaves like a single structural unit. It will not sag between supports, will not shift under load, and cut edges stay exactly where you put them. This is why <a href="/products/welded-wire-mesh/" class="text-theme-primary underline">welded wire mesh panels</a> dominate fencing, security cages, machine guards, and concrete-adjacent applications.
      </p>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4 leading-relaxed">
        The weakness is the weld itself. Every intersection is a heat-affected zone and a stress concentration point. Under repeated impact or vibration—think of a trailer floor, a rock-crusher guard, or a cattle panel taking kicks—weld points can fatigue and snap one by one. Welded mesh also cannot be made with fine openings: below roughly 6mm, welding thin wires at production speed becomes impractical, so welded mesh is structurally excluded from fine filtration.
      </p>

      <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-theme-primary mb-3">Woven Wire Mesh: Strengths and Weaknesses</h3>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4 leading-relaxed">
        Woven mesh is produced on looms where warp and weft wires interlace in a pattern (plain, twill, or dutch weave). No heat is applied, so wires keep their full mechanical properties and apertures stay uniform—critical when you need to capture a 150-micron particle and let a 100-micron particle pass. Woven <a href="/products/stainless-steel-wire-mesh/" class="text-theme-primary underline">stainless steel wire mesh</a> is the default for filtration, test sieving, and particle classification for exactly this reason.
      </p>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4 leading-relaxed">
        The weaknesses come from flexibility. Woven mesh drapes like fabric, so it needs a frame, tensioning, or support layers to span any distance. Cut edges can unravel if not finished with a selvage, clamped in a frame, or welded into a finished element. And because the weave moves microscopically under load, woven mesh is the wrong choice for guarding or fencing—impacts deform the aperture rather than resisting them.
      </p>

      <div class="bg-theme-secondary bg-opacity-10 border border-theme rounded-lg p-6">
        <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-theme-primary mb-2">Honest Tradeoff Summary</h3>
        <ul class="text-theme-secondary space-y-2 text-sm">
          <li><strong>Welded wins on:</strong> rigidity, edge stability, large-opening cost, installation speed, structural loads</li>
          <li><strong>Woven wins on:</strong> aperture precision, opening range (20 microns and below), conformability, filtration duty</li>
          <li><strong>Welded loses on:</strong> weld fatigue under vibration, no fine-opening capability</li>
          <li><strong>Woven loses on:</strong> needs support/framing, unraveling edges, cost at very heavy gauges</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </section>

