    <!-- FAQ -->
    <section id="faq" class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-theme-primary mb-6">6. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

      <div class="space-y-4">
        <details class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg">
          <summary class="p-4 font-semibold text-theme-primary cursor-pointer">Which is stronger: welded or woven wire mesh?</summary>
          <div class="p-4 pt-0 text-theme-secondary">
            For structural loads, welded mesh is stronger—it distributes force across fused intersections and holds its shape. For tensile and flexural duty (vibration, draping, pulley systems), woven mesh is stronger because it has no brittle weld points to fail. "Stronger" depends entirely on the load type.
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg">
          <summary class="p-4 font-semibold text-theme-primary cursor-pointer">Can I use welded wire mesh for filtration?</summary>
          <div class="p-4 pt-0 text-theme-secondary">
            Only for very coarse screening (particles larger than ~6mm). Welded mesh cannot be produced with the fine, uniform apertures filtration requires. For anything finer, use woven mesh—see our <a href="/blog/how-to-choose-wire-mesh-for-filtration/" class="text-theme-primary underline">filtration selection guide</a>.
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg">
          <summary class="p-4 font-semibold text-theme-primary cursor-pointer">What is the smallest opening available in each type?</summary>
          <div class="p-4 pt-0 text-theme-secondary">
            Welded mesh practical minimum is around 6mm × 6mm. Woven mesh goes down to 635 mesh (~20 microns) in plain weave and 1 micron in dutch weave. If you need sub-micron filtration, consider sintered mesh or membrane filters instead.
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg">
          <summary class="p-4 font-semibold text-theme-primary cursor-pointer">Is welded or woven wire mesh more expensive?</summary>
          <div class="p-4 pt-0 text-theme-secondary">
            At large openings in galvanized steel, welded is cheaper ($3–6/m² vs. $4–9/m² for comparable woven). At fine openings, woven is the only option, so the comparison is moot. In stainless steel, mid-range woven mesh ($8–18/m²) is often comparable to or cheaper than stainless welded panels ($15–28/m²) because welded uses much heavier wire.
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg">
          <summary class="p-4 font-semibold text-theme-primary cursor-pointer">Can I cut woven wire mesh without it unraveling?</summary>
          <div class="p-4 pt-0 text-theme-secondary">
            Cut woven mesh edges will fray unless you finish them. Options: order mesh with a selvage edge (factory-finished), clamp the edge in a frame, fold and crimp the border, or spot-weld/tack the perimeter. For loose pieces, fold the cut edge 6–10mm over and crimp it.
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg">
          <summary class="p-4 font-semibold text-theme-primary cursor-pointer">Which should I choose for a security fence?</summary>
          <div class="p-4 pt-0 text-theme-secondary">
            Welded, almost always. Choose 50×50mm or 75×75mm openings with 3–4mm wire, hot-dip galvanized (60+ g/m²) plus PVC coating for 15+ year service life. Woven chain-link is cheaper upfront but is climbable and deforms under impact—welded panels are the standard for perimeter security.
          </div>
        </details>
      </div>
    </section>

